# =============================================================================
# Edge gateway — bnna.dev
#
# Pattern:
#   - One shared snippet (edge_auth) carries TLS contact and the edge-key
#     guard clause. Adding a third upstream is a three-line site block.
#
# Guard-clause mechanics:
#   - Caddy sorts directives by its internal order, where `respond` runs
#     AFTER `reverse_proxy` — so a bare matched respond would never fire.
#     Wrapping the 401 in `route` pins evaluation where it is written:
#     bad keys terminate here, valid keys fall through to the proxy.
#
# Body cap — REMOVED 2026-08-15:
#   - The snippet used to carry `request_body { max_size 8MB }`, sized against
#     a text-only workload (a 184K-token prompt is roughly 0.75 MB of JSON).
#     OCR traffic sends base64 images inline in the chat payload, which blows
#     past 8MB on ordinary requests, so the cap is gone entirely and Caddy no
#     longer bounds body size at any site.
#   - What still bounds a request: `--context-length 169000` on each SGLang
#     replica. Over-length prompts are rejected by the engine after
#     tokenisation instead of by byte count at the edge, so a pathological
#     payload now costs a transfer and a tokenise before it is refused.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global options
#
# The global `metrics` option turns on Prometheus instrumentation for the HTTP
# servers. Without it the only thing on /metrics is Caddy's own process and
# config gauges — no request counts, no status codes, no latency histograms.
# (The nested `servers { metrics }` spelling is deprecated and disappears in
# the next major version — use this one.)
#
# `per_host` adds a host label, which is what separates inference traffic from
# embedder traffic in the same series. It is off by default because an
# arbitrary Host header would otherwise mint unbounded label values; Caddy
# guards that by only recording configured hosts and bucketing everything else
# under `_other`. Both our sites are HTTPS with real certificates, so the
# label set is bounded by this file: two hosts plus `_other`.
#
# The admin API is deliberately left at its default — localhost:2019, inside
# the container only. It can rewrite Caddy's whole config, so it must not be
# reachable from a docker network. Metrics are served separately below.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
	metrics {
		per_host
	}
}

(edge_auth) {
	# ACME registration contact for certificate issuance
	tls contact@bogdanna.dev

	# Reject anything without the edge key before it reaches the proxy
	@bad_key not header Authorization "Bearer {$EDGE_API_KEY}"
	route @bad_key {
		respond "Unauthorized" 401
	}
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qwen3.6-27B — SGLang router on the A100 node
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
qw36-27b.bnna.dev {
	import edge_auth

	reverse_proxy qwen36-27b-router:8000 {
		# Swap the edge credential for the internal SGLang key
		header_up Authorization "Bearer {$SGLANG_API_KEY}"

		# SSE streaming: disable response buffering entirely
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B — TEI (CPU)
# NOTE: after the network split, the TEI container must join the shared
# `edge` network so the name `qwen3-emb` resolves from this Caddy.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
qw3-emb.bnna.dev {
	import edge_auth

	reverse_proxy qwen3-emb:80
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qwen3.8-27B via the Higress gateway — PAID / EXTERNAL consumers
#
# Deliberately NOT importing edge_auth: that snippet asserts the single shared
# EDGE_API_KEY, which is exactly what this hostname replaces. Identity here is
# per-consumer (Higress key-auth) and budgets are token quotas (ai-quota).
#
# The team keeps using qw36-27b.bnna.dev above — straight to the router, no
# gateway, no Redis, no quota. That is deliberate: ai-quota has no fail-open, so
# if the ledger is unavailable every consumer on THIS hostname gets a 403. The
# direct hostname is the standing escape hatch.
#
# Rehearsed in full on a throwaway Caddy on the edge network (no published
# ports, tls internal) before landing here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
qw38-27b-gw.duckdns.org {
	tls contact@bogdanna.dev

	# Bounds the PROMPT half of a request's cost. A byte cap is the only lever
	# that exists before tokenisation, and it is what makes overdraft bounded:
	# ai-quota's gate is `> 0` and its DECRBY lands afterwards, so one request
	# can always cross zero. 1MB comfortably fits the served 169k context
	# (~0.75MB of JSON) while refusing absurd payloads. The output half is
	# capped separately by request-validation in the gateway (max_tokens 8192),
	# so worst-case overdraft is ~one full-context request, not unbounded.
	#
	# NOTE this is per-site. qw36-27b.bnna.dev deliberately has NO cap, because
	# OCR traffic sends base64 images inline and blew past the old global 8MB
	# limit. Paid traffic does not get that allowance.
	request_body {
		max_size 1MB
	}

	# Only the OpenAI surface is forwarded. Higress serves its console landing
	# page on GET / of the gateway port — a seeded route we do not own — and
	# without this it would be publicly visible. Everything else dies here
	# rather than relying on Higress's own route allowlist alone.
	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy higress:8080 {
			# key-auth ADDS X-Mse-Consumer rather than replacing it, so a
			# client-supplied value arrives alongside the authenticated one.
			# Measured: the real identity wins — but by Envoy's duplicate-header
			# semantics, not by anything key-auth guarantees. Strip it here so
			# the question never arises.
			header_up -X-Mse-Consumer

			# SSE: no response buffering. ai-quota reads token usage from the
			# final stream frame, so buffering breaks metering as well as the
			# client experience. Verified unbuffered through handle_response:
			# TTFB 0.15s against 2.38s total.
			flush_interval -1

			# Give the gateway's denials an OpenAI-shaped body. key-auth and
			# ai-quota answer text/plain, and an SDK shows a JSON parse failure
			# or nothing at all against a bare string. This cannot be done in
			# Higress: both are wasm LOCAL replies, and the custom-response
			# wasm plugin never sees another plugin's local reply — verified,
			# it loads into the filter chain but does not fire.
			#
			# Status codes pass through unchanged so client retry logic still
			# behaves. SGLang's own 400s are NOT rewritten — the engine already
			# returns OpenAI-shaped errors and its messages are more useful
			# than anything we could substitute.
			@quota status 403
			handle_response @quota {
				header Content-Type "application/json; charset=utf-8"
				respond `{"error":{"message":"Token quota exhausted for this API key, or the key is not permitted on this route. Contact the operator to top up.","type":"insufficient_quota","param":null,"code":"insufficient_quota"}}` 403
			}

			@badkey status 401
			handle_response @badkey {
				header Content-Type "application/json; charset=utf-8"
				respond `{"error":{"message":"Incorrect API key provided. Send it as an Authorization: Bearer header.","type":"invalid_request_error","param":null,"code":"invalid_api_key"}}` 401
			}

			# 422 is request-validation's alone; the engine never returns it.
			# The number below MUST match the `maximum` in
			# ../higress/config/wasmplugins/request-validation.yaml — this is
			# the copy the customer actually reads.
			@toobig status 422
			handle_response @toobig {
				header Content-Type "application/json; charset=utf-8"
				respond `{"error":{"message":"max_tokens exceeds the per-request limit of 70000 for this gateway.","type":"invalid_request_error","param":"max_tokens","code":"max_tokens_too_large"}}` 422
			}
		}
	}

	# Default deny. No hint that a gateway or console lives behind this name.
	handle {
		respond "Not Found" 404
	}

	# request_body aborts before proxying, so its 413 never reaches the
	# handle_response blocks above. Shape it here instead.
	handle_errors {
		header Content-Type "application/json; charset=utf-8"
		respond `{"error":{"message":"Request rejected by the gateway. If the prompt is very large, reduce it: the request body limit is 1MB.","type":"invalid_request_error","param":null,"code":"request_rejected"}}` {err.status_code}
	}
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Grafana — observability UI, PUBLIC over HTTPS
#
# Why it is public at all: the Higress console can only embed a dashboard from
# a URL the BROWSER can reach, and browsers refuse to frame a page that sends
# `X-Frame-Options: deny`. Dropping that header requires the session cookie to
# be SameSite=None, which browsers only accept together with Secure, which
# requires HTTPS. So the iframe forces a real certificate.
#
# NOT importing edge_auth on purpose. Grafana has its own login, and a second
# Bearer check in front would break both the browser login flow and the iframe.
# The admin password was rotated from a 6-character value identical to the
# username before this hostname was created — see .env.
#
# Anonymous viewing is deliberately OFF (docker-compose.metrics.yml): these
# dashboards carry per-consumer token usage, i.e. customer names and spend.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
qw38-27b-grafana.duckdns.org {
	tls contact@bogdanna.dev

	reverse_proxy qwen36-27b-grafana:3000 {
		# Grafana Live uses websockets; Caddy upgrades them automatically, but
		# streaming panels also want unbuffered responses.
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Metrics exposition — internal only
#
# A port-only site address means plain HTTP with no automatic TLS and no
# hostname matching, so this never appears as a public endpoint: port 2020 is
# not in the compose `ports:` list, so it exists only on the `edge` docker
# network where Prometheus scrapes it.
#
# Deliberately NOT importing edge_auth: that snippet asserts a Bearer header
# and would 401 the scraper, and the edge key has no business being in
# prometheus.yml. Network isolation is the control here, same as every other
# exporter in this stack.
#
# This is the only telemetry that sees the edge itself — TLS handshake
# failures and 401s from bad edge keys are invisible to the SGLang and router
# metrics, because those requests never reach them. It is also the only place
# request body sizes are observed now that nothing caps them.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
:2020 {
	metrics
}
